 ## Overview
-DOF Gaussian Rasterization is a differentiable rendering method for 3D Gaussians that supports adjustable depth-of-field (DoF), enabling refocusing, defocus rendering, and blur removal by incorporating circle-of-confusion (CoC) alpha and depth-dependent scaling [S1, unofficial]. It extends standard 3D Gaussian Splatting by allowing the focal distance to be varied post-training, decoupling the rendered focus plane from the camera's optical center [S1, unofficial].
+DOF Gaussian Rasterization is a differentiable rasterization method for 3D Gaussians that supports adjustable depth-of-field, enabling refocusing, defocus rendering, and blur removal by incorporating circle-of-confusion (CoC) alpha and depth-dependent scaling [S1, unofficial]. It extends standard 3D Gaussian Splatting by allowing the focal distance to be varied post-training, decoupling the rendered focus plane from the camera's optical center [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates as a CUDA-accelerated rasterizer that processes a set of 3D Gaussians into a 2D image. It differs from standard splatting by computing a depth-dependent alpha mask (CoC alpha) that modulates the contribution of each Gaussian based on its distance from the specified focal plane [S1, unofficial].
